# Unreal's conventions, so engine code and project code read the same. Nothing
# in here is worth an argument, which is the point of automating it.
#
# **This describes the house style; it does not yet enforce it.** The options
# below the first block were fitted to the code as written, which got the whole
# tree to within about 2,000 lines of clang-format's output -- close enough to
# show the style is real and consistent, not close enough to be a gate. CI used
# to run `clang-format --dry-run --Werror` over everything and had therefore
# failed on every commit since the check was added, because the code has never
# been run through the formatter at all.
#
# Adopting it is a task rather than a tidy-up: the reformat pushes three files
# that already sit at 481, 484 and 498 lines over the 500-line limit, and the
# standard's own answer to that is "ask what two things the file is doing".
# Until somebody does that, this file is for editors. To see where it stands:
#
#     pip install clang-format==23.1.0
#     find client/Source -name '*.cpp' -o -name '*.h' | xargs clang-format -i
#
# Pin the version. clang-format reflows differently between major releases, so
# an unpinned check turns "the code is formatted" into "the code is formatted
# the way whichever version the runner installed today would have done it".
BasedOnStyle: LLVM
Language: Cpp
UseTab: Always
TabWidth: 4
IndentWidth: 4
ColumnLimit: 100
BreakBeforeBraces: Allman
AllowShortFunctionsOnASingleLine: Inline
AllowShortIfStatementsOnASingleLine: false
AllowShortLoopsOnASingleLine: false
NamespaceIndentation: All
AccessModifierOffset: -4
PointerAlignment: Left
SortIncludes: CaseSensitive
IncludeBlocks: Preserve
AlignAfterOpenBracket: AlwaysBreak
BinPackParameters: false
BinPackArguments: false
ContinuationIndentWidth: 4
FixNamespaceComments: false
PenaltyReturnTypeOnItsOwnLine: 10000
PenaltyBreakAssignment: 1000
AlignOperands: DontAlign
BreakBeforeBinaryOperators: NonAssignment
